WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:06.650
What can I tell you? I read in the history
books that the strike was organised and

2
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.110
led by the communists,

3
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:17.210
that it was political, that the hero Vasile
Roaită sounded the siren and was shot for it.

4
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:21.610
A whopping great lie. The siren was
sounded by a man called Vasile Negrea.

5
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:28.210
He was a worker too, and nothing happened to him
– maybe he got a bit knocked about in the crush,

6
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:35.504
but nothing more. What's certain is that
after the war Gheorghiu-Dej wanted to take

7
00:00:35.554 --> 00:00:39.810
the credit for organising
and leading the strike.

8
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:47.893
He tracked down this Vasile Negrea and told
him to keep quiet and let people say it

9
00:00:47.943 --> 00:00:51.010
was Vasile Roaită who'd done it.

10
00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:58.210
Vasile Roaită really did die. I don't know
whether he was shot or died in the crush.

11
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.963
What's certain is that he was
the youngest of the workers.

12
00:01:03.013 --> 00:01:09.110
He was an apprentice there, in the
paint shop, I think – it doesn't matter.

13
00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.116
And the idea that he was in
the Communist Youth isn't true.

14
00:01:14.166 --> 00:01:17.510
He had nothing to do with that struggle.

15
00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.097
So Gheorghiu-Dej tried
to take all the credit.

16
00:01:20.147 --> 00:01:22.910
And he managed it. That's
how history recorded it.

17
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:31.966
Then Ceaușescu, in the Ceaușescu years
after '65, when he turned his back on those

18
00:01:32.016 --> 00:01:35.610
before him, changed things a bit.

19
00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:44.487
He didn't keep all the lies, but he still
let people think that he too had been

20
00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:46.510
jailed around '35,

21
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.964
that he'd been part of the movement as well.

22
00:01:51.014 --> 00:01:54.810
But he had nothing to do with Grivița.

23
00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:59.600
He was very young. I knew a few things from
my family – not just this business with

24
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:04.910
my father; there were other things too, but
they're not what we're here to talk about today.

25
00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.610
I was born, grew up and
still live near the station.

26
00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:15.110
I was born and first lived on Știrbei
Vodă, opposite the Cuibul cu Barză church.

27
00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:24.233
Then, in my twenties, I moved to Titulescu
– still close by, three stops from here,

28
00:02:24.283 --> 00:02:27.310
one stop from the town hall.

29
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.689
And since 2020 I've lived here, where I
am now, between the Grant Bridge and the

30
00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:32.510
Basarab Bridge.

31
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:40.097
The Hipodrom Park used to be where the
so-called Casa Radio is now – where Ceaușescu

32
00:02:40.147 --> 00:02:43.710
held his last parade, on 23 August 1989.

33
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.948
There was a park there, a children's park.

34
00:02:47.998 --> 00:02:54.910
And because I lived on Știrbei Vodă,
about one stop from the station,

35
00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:01.610
that's the very place where I
used to play football. Anyway.

36
00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:05.965
The Dâmbovița embankment
– I miss the old one.

37
00:03:06.015 --> 00:03:13.538
The river's been channelled now and it
looks very nice, but back then there were

38
00:03:13.588 --> 00:03:15.810
trees, there was nature.

39
00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:22.610
I miss those places. I miss the old
Herăstrău Park, and Cișmigiu Park,

40
00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:26.510
because I spent my childhood
round there too, close by.

41
00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:34.510
Not to mention the station. The Grant
Bridge has changed a lot from what it was.

42
00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:44.310
I remember the old one, the iron one, built by
a famous engineer called Grant – an Englishman.

43
00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.279
Not 'grand' but Grant, with a t.

44
00:03:46.329 --> 00:03:51.387
Lots of people got it mixed up and said
'grand', which means 'big' in French, as in

45
00:03:51.437 --> 00:03:52.310
the big bridge.

46
00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:58.401
It's true it was the biggest bridge at the
time, or one of the biggest, but it was

47
00:03:58.451 --> 00:04:00.110
named after him, Grant.

48
00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:09.310
It was built between the wars. It was very
interesting – made of iron, and made in Romania.

49
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.426
It had two lanes, one going and one coming.

50
00:04:13.476 --> 00:04:20.510
It wasn't very big compared to what's
there now, but it was really useful.

51
00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.692
Back to Grivița, since you
asked me what ties me to it.

52
00:04:24.742 --> 00:04:27.810
Where that lady said the club used to be,

53
00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:32.865
where the MedLife clinic is now, where she
says the canteen was – actually, I don't

54
00:04:32.915 --> 00:04:34.510
think it matters any more –

55
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:40.630
that's where they held parties and
get-togethers for young people.

56
00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.710
I even organised a few parties there myself.

57
00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.293
Anyone who says things were good back then…

58
00:04:48.343 --> 00:04:51.710
Of course there are things we don't like.

59
00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:55.735
We don't like the corruption,
the dishonesty, the unfairness.

60
00:04:55.785 --> 00:05:01.410
There's a lot we don't like: we don't like
the government, we don't like its policies.

61
00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:08.559
But compared with how it was then, with
the dictatorship, the poverty and the lack

62
00:05:08.609 --> 00:05:11.610
of freedom – there's no comparison.

